cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "Erdős–Straus conjecture: blind shifts, conditional results and computations"
authors:
  - family-names: "Casafranca"
    given-names: "Andy"
version: "1.0.0"
date-released: "2026-11-26"
repository-code: "https://github.com/AndyRCC/erdos-straus"
license: "MIT"
abstract: >-
  Notes on the (still open) Erdős–Straus conjecture 4/n = 1/x + 1/y + 1/z.
  Theorem: for every c ≡ 3 (mod 4), the number of primes n ≤ N for which the
  shift c fails (no Type I or Type II solution with first denominator
  x = (n+c)/4) is ≍ N(log N)^(-3/2). This extends Dahan's Theorem 4.15 (c = 7,
  arXiv:2608.24035) and settles the case c = 3; it follows from
  Fuchs–Hsu–Rickards–Schindler–Stange (arXiv:2504.20289) applied to the form of
  discriminant −3. Conditional results: Dickson's prime k-tuples conjecture
  implies that the first working shift m(p) is unbounded, and Bunyakovsky's
  conjecture for one explicit quadratic, built from the congruent-number curve
  y² = x³ − 231²x, implies m(p) ≥ 15 infinitely often. Computations: Dahan's
  sieve experiments replicated and extended to 10^9; the three exceptions
  (193, 2521, 66529) of the Aigner Type II slice with a = 1 below 10^9; m(p)
  computed exactly for all 128 671 219 hard primes below 10^11; a targeted
  search above 10^18. The note is in Spanish. This is not a proof of the
  conjecture. Code under MIT; note and data under CC BY 4.0.
keywords:
  - "Erdős–Straus conjecture"
  - "Egyptian fractions"
  - "number theory"
  - "sieve methods"
  - "quadratic forms"
  - "reproducibility"
